        Statistical genetics formulates phenotype as a mathematical function of genotypes, environmental variance, and unobserved ancestry structures:
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Population Foundations:</strong> Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium ($p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = 1$), Wright-Fisher genetic drift, and Kingman's coalescent model explain allele frequency dynamics.
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Variance Decomposition:</strong> Total phenotypic variance partitions into additive genetic, dominance, epistatic interaction, gene-environment, and environmental noise (<Katex tex="V_P = V_A + V_D + V_I + V_{G \times E} + V_E" />).
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Linkage Disequilibrium:</strong> Non-random allele co-inheritance (<Katex tex="r^2, D'" />) creates correlated summary statistics, necessitating fine-mapping and LDSC.
        <br/>
