    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="ch1">
      <h2>1. Foundations of Population Genetics & The Infinitesimal Model</h2>

      <h3>Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium (HWE)</h3>
      <p>
        In an infinitely large, panmictic (random-mating) diploid population with non-overlapping generations and in the absence of mutation, migration, and natural selection, allele and genotype frequencies remain constant over generations.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a biallelic locus with alleles <Katex tex="A" /> and <Katex tex="a" /> having frequencies <Katex tex="p = P(A)" /> and <Katex tex="q = P(a) = 1 - p" />, random fusion of gametes yields the genotype probabilities:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Hardy-Weinberg Genotype Proportions</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="P(AA) = p^2, \quad P(Aa) = 2pq, \quad P(aa) = q^2 \implies p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = (p + q)^2 = 1" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          Degrees of Freedom: <Katex tex="\text{df} = 3 - 1 - 1 = 1" /> (3 genotype classes minus 1 for fixed total sample size minus 1 for estimated allele frequency <Katex tex="p" />).
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        In GWAS quality control, deviations from HWE are tested using Pearson's <Katex tex="\chi^2" /> goodness-of-fit test or Fisher's exact test. Significant HWE departures (<Katex tex="P < 10^{-6}" /> in controls) typically indicate <strong>genotyping errors</strong> (such as batch-specific probe hybridization failures or allele dropout), strong natural selection, or severe population stratification.
      </p>

      <h3>Wright-Fisher Model & Genetic Drift</h3>
      <p>
        In finite populations of size <Katex tex="N" /> (containing <Katex tex="2N" /> gametes), sampling variance causes allele frequencies to fluctuate randomly across generations—a stochastic process known as <strong>genetic drift</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Wright-Fisher Transition Probability & Heterozygosity Loss</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="P(k \mid j) = \binom{2N}{k} \left(\frac{j}{2N}\right)^k \left(1 - \frac{j}{2N}\right)^{2N - k}, \quad H_t = H_0 \left(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\right)^t \approx H_0 e^{-t / (2N_e)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="P(k \mid j)" /> = Probability that an allele with <Katex tex="j" /> copies in generation <Katex tex="t" /> has <Katex tex="k" /> copies in generation <Katex tex="t+1" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="H_t = 2p_t q_t" /> = Expected heterozygosity at generation <Katex tex="t" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="N_e" /> = Effective population size.
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Under neutral genetic drift, the probability that a neutral allele eventually reaches fixation equals its current frequency: <Katex tex="P(\text{fixation}) = p_0" />. For a new neutral mutation arising in a single copy (<Katex tex="p_0 = 1/(2N)" />), the average time to fixation conditional on fixing is <Katex tex="4N_e" /> generations.
      </p>

      <h3>Coalescent Theory & Tajima's D</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Kingman's Coalescent (1982)</strong> models the genealogy of <Katex tex="k" /> sampled lineages looking backward in time. The time <Katex tex="T_k" /> during which there are <Katex tex="k" /> distinct ancestors is exponentially distributed with rate <Katex tex="\lambda_k = \binom{k}{2} / (2N_e)" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Expected Time to Most Recent Common Ancestor (TMRCA)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[T_{\text{MRCA}}] = \sum_{k=2}^{n} \mathbb{E}[T_k] = \sum_{k=2}^{n} \frac{4N_e}{k(k-1)} = 4N_e \left(1 - \frac{1}{n}\right) \approx 4N_e \text{ generations}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>Tajima's D statistic (1989)</strong> compares two estimators of the population mutation parameter <Katex tex="\theta = 4N_e \mu" />: the average pairwise nucleotide diversity (<Katex tex="\hat{\theta}_\pi" />) and Watterson's estimator based on the number of segregating sites (<Katex tex="\hat{\theta}_S = S / a_1" />):
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Tajima's D Neutrality Test</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="D = \frac{\hat{\theta}_\pi - \hat{\theta}_S}{\sqrt{\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat{\theta}_\pi - \hat{\theta}_S)}}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="D \approx 0" />: Neutral evolution &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="D < 0" />: Excess of rare alleles (positive selective sweep or population expansion) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="D > 0" />: Excess of intermediate-frequency alleles (balancing selection or population bottleneck).
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>The Fisher 1918 Synthesis & The Infinitesimal Model</h3>
      <p>
        In a landmark paper, <strong>Ronald A. Fisher (1918)</strong> reconciled Mendelian particulate genetics with Galtonian biometry. Fisher proved that if a trait is governed by an infinite number of Mendelian loci (<Katex tex="M \to \infty" />), each with an infinitesimally small additive effect <Katex tex="\beta_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2/M)" />, the sum of these independent alleles converges—by the <strong>Central Limit Theorem</strong>—to a continuous Gaussian phenotypic distribution in the population:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Central Limit Convergence of Polygenic Traits</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="y_i = \mu + \sum_{j=1}^{M} \beta_j X_{ij} + \epsilon_i \xrightarrow{M \to \infty} y_i \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, V_P)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="X_{ij} \in \{0, 1, 2\}" /> = Genotype dosage at SNP <Katex tex="j" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\beta_j" /> = Additive allelic effect size &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\epsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, V_E)" /> = Environmental noise.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Complete Phenotypic Variance Partitioning</h3>
      <p>
        Total phenotypic variance <Katex tex="V_P = \text{Var}(y)" /> decomposes into genetic, interaction, and environmental components:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Master Variance Decomposition</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="V_P = V_G + V_E + 2\text{Cov}(G, E) = V_A + V_D + V_I + V_{G \times E} + 2\text{Cov}(G, E) + V_E" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="V_A" /> = Additive genetic variance (linear sum of allele substitution effects) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="V_D" /> = Dominance variance (intra-locus allelic interactions) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="V_I = V_{AA} + V_{AD} + V_{DD}" /> = Epistatic variance (inter-locus interactions) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="V_{G \times E}" /> = Gene-by-environment interaction variance.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Narrow-Sense vs. Broad-Sense Heritability & The Breeder's Equation</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Heritability Formulations & The Breeder's Equation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="h^2 = \frac{V_A}{V_P} \quad (\text{Narrow-sense}), \qquad H^2 = \frac{V_G}{V_P} = \frac{V_A + V_D + V_I}{V_P} \quad (\text{Broad-sense}), \qquad R = h^2 S" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <strong>Breeder's Equation (Lush 1937):</strong> <Katex tex="R = \mu' - \mu" /> (Evolutionary response to selection) = <Katex tex="h^2" /> (Narrow-sense heritability) <Katex tex="\times S = \mu_s - \mu" /> (Selection differential).
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Dedicated Deep Dive: Population Genetics, Coalescent & Infinitesimal Models</h3>
        <p>
          Explore the first-principles mathematical derivations of Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, Wright-Fisher genetic drift, Kingman's coalescent TMRCA, Tajima's D neutrality metric, and Fisher's 1918 infinitesimal synthesis.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/statgen-population-infinitesimal/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Read Full Deep Dive on Population Genetics</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="ch2">
      <h2>2. Linkage Disequilibrium (LD) & Chromosomal Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Linkage Disequilibrium (LD)</strong> is the non-random association of alleles at different loci across chromosomes in a population.
      </p>

      <h3>Mathematical Quantification of LD</h3>
      <p>
        Consider two biallelic loci with alleles <Katex tex="A/a" /> and <Katex tex="B/b" /> having marginal frequencies <Katex tex="p_A, p_a, p_B, p_b" /> and haplotype frequency <Katex tex="p_{AB}" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Coefficient of Linkage Disequilibrium (D)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="D = p_{AB} - p_A p_B = p_{AB} p_{ab} - p_{Ab} p_{aB}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Lewontin's Normalized D' (1964) & Squared Correlation Coefficient r² (Hill & Robertson 1968)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="D' = \frac{D}{D_{\max}}, \quad D_{\max} = \begin{cases} \min(p_A p_b, p_a p_B) & \text{if } D > 0 \\ \min(p_A p_B, p_a p_b) & \text{if } D < 0 \end{cases}, \qquad r^2 = \frac{D^2}{p_A (1 - p_A) p_B (1 - p_B)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="|D'| = 1" /> indicates complete linkage (absence of one of the 4 possible haplotypes, meaning no historical recombination has separated the loci) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="r^2 \in [0, 1]" /> measures statistical correlation, determining tag SNP imputation accuracy and GWAS statistical power.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Recombination Mechanics & Exponential Decay of LD</h3>
      <p>
        Under meiotic recombination occurring at rate <Katex tex="\theta" /> per generation, LD decays exponentially over time:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Decay of Linkage Disequilibrium Over Generations</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="D_t = D_0 (1 - \theta)^t \approx D_0 e^{-\theta t}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-biology">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="activity" size={15} /> Biological Drivers of Recombination: PRDM9 & Hotspots
        </div>
        <p>
          In humans and mice, meiotic recombination does not occur uniformly. Over 80% of crossovers are concentrated in narrow 1–2 kb <strong>recombination hotspots</strong> positioned by <strong>PRDM9</strong>, a zinc-finger histone methyltransferase that binds specific 13-mer sequence motifs (<Katex tex="\text{CCNCCNTNNCCNC}" />), depositing H3K4me3 and H3K36me3 to recruit SPO11 for programmed double-strand breaks (DSBs).
          <br/>
          This punctate recombination landscape creates discrete <strong>haplotype blocks</strong> (regions of high internal <Katex tex="r^2 > 0.8" /> spanning 10–100 kb) separated by recombination hotspots.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Dedicated Deep Dive: Linkage Disequilibrium, Recombination & PRDM9</h3>
        <p>
          Master the mathematical quantification of LD (D, D', r²), exponential decay dynamics over generations, PRDM9 zinc-finger sequence motifs, SPO11 double-strand breaks, and human haplotype block structure.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/statgen-linkage-disequilibrium/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Read Full Deep Dive on Linkage Disequilibrium</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="ch3">
      <h2>3. Heritability Estimation: From Twins to Genomic REML (GREML)</h2>

      <h3>Falconer's Twin Formula</h3>
      <p>
        Classic quantitative genetics estimates heritability by comparing phenotypic correlations between Monozygotic twins (<Katex tex="r_{\text{MZ}}" />, sharing 100% of their DNA) and Dizygotic twins (<Katex tex="r_{\text{DZ}}" />, sharing on average 50% of segregating alleles):
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Falconer's Heritability Formula (1960)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="r_{\text{MZ}} = h^2 + c^2, \quad r_{\text{DZ}} = \frac{1}{2}h^2 + c^2 \implies h^2 = 2(r_{\text{MZ}} - r_{\text{DZ}})" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="c^2" /> = Variance contributed by shared environmental factors between twins.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>The "Missing Heritability" Dilemma</h3>
      <p>
        When the first wave of GWAS arrived (2007–2010), a major crisis emerged: genome-wide significant hits (<Katex tex="P < 5 \times 10^{-8}" />) explained only a tiny fraction of twin-estimated heritability (e.g. for human height, twin <Katex tex="h^2 \approx 80\%" />, but significant GWAS loci explained only <Katex tex="\approx 5\%" />).
      </p>

      <h3>The Genomic Relatedness Matrix (GRM) & GCTA</h3>
      <p>
        In a breakthrough paper, <strong>Yang et al. (2010) (GCTA)</strong> resolved this dilemma by demonstrating that the "missing" heritability was not absent—it was scattered across hundreds of thousands of common SNPs with effect sizes too small to achieve genome-wide significance individually.
      </p>
      <p>
        Yang et al. constructed the <strong>Genomic Relatedness Matrix (GRM)</strong> <Katex tex="\mathbf{A}" /> from <Katex tex="M" /> genotyped SNPs across <Katex tex="N" /> unrelated individuals:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Yang et al. (2010) Genomic Relatedness Estimator</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="A_{ik} = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{j=1}^{M} \frac{(X_{ij} - 2p_j)(X_{kj} - 2p_j)}{2p_j (1 - p_j)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="X_{ij} \in \{0, 1, 2\}" /> = Genotype dosage of individual <Katex tex="i" /> at SNP <Katex tex="j" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="p_j" /> = Sample minor allele frequency at SNP <Katex tex="j" />.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Genomic-REML (GREML) Variance Component Estimation</h3>
      <p>
        GCTA fits a linear mixed model with random polygenic effects:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">GREML Linear Mixed Model & Variance Structure</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta} + \mathbf{g} + \mathbf{e}, \quad \mathbf{g} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{A}\sigma_g^2), \quad \mathbf{e} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{I}\sigma_e^2) \implies \mathbf{V} = \mathbf{A}\sigma_g^2 + \mathbf{I}\sigma_e^2" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="h^2_{\text{SNP}} = \frac{\sigma_g^2}{\sigma_g^2 + \sigma_e^2}" /> captures the total phenotypic variance tagged by all common SNPs simultaneously (<Katex tex="\approx 45\text{--}50\%" /> for height).
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Dedicated Deep Dive: Heritability Estimation & GREML (GCTA)</h3>
        <p>
          Deep dive into classical twin models, Falconer's formula, the missing heritability dilemma, and Yang et al. (2010) GCTA Genomic Relatedness Matrix (GRM) variance component estimation via AI-REML.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/statgen-heritability-greml/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Read Full Deep Dive on GREML & Heritability</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="ch4">
      <h2>4. Summary-Statistics Heritability: LD Score Regression (LDSC & S-LDSC)</h2>
      <p>
        GREML requires individual-level genotype matrices (<Katex tex="O(N^2)" /> memory), making it computationally intractable and ethically restricted for millions of biobank participants. <strong>LD Score Regression (LDSC, Bulik-Sullivan et al. 2015)</strong> estimates <Katex tex="h^2" /> directly from public GWAS summary statistics.
      </p>

      <h3>Defining the LD Score</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Single-Variant LD Score</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\ell_j = \sum_{k=1}^{M} r_{jk}^2" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          Sums the squared correlation of variant <Katex tex="j" /> with all other <Katex tex="M" /> variants in the genome (precomputed from reference panels like 1000 Genomes).
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>The Fundamental LDSC Theorem & Mathematical Proof</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Bulik-Sullivan et al. (2015) Fundamental LDSC Expectation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\chi^2_j] = 1 + \frac{N h^2}{M} \ell_j + N a" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\chi^2_j = z_j^2" /> = GWAS chi-squared association statistic &emsp;|&emsp;
          Slope = <Katex tex="\frac{N h^2}{M}" /> (estimates SNP heritability) &emsp;|&emsp;
          Intercept = <Katex tex="1 + Na" /> (measures confounding from population stratification and cryptic relatedness).
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> Mathematical Proof of the LDSC Theorem
        </div>
        <p>
          Let the true polygenic model be <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}" /> with standardized genotypes <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[X_{ij}] = 0, \text{Var}(X_{ij}) = 1" />, where true effects satisfy <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\beta_k] = 0, \text{Var}(\beta_k) = \frac{h^2}{M}" />.
          <br/><br/>
          The marginal OLS estimator for SNP <Katex tex="j" /> is <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_j = \frac{1}{N}\mathbf{x}_j^T \mathbf{y} = \sum_{k=1}^M r_{jk} \beta_k + \frac{1}{N}\mathbf{x}_j^T \boldsymbol{\epsilon}" />.
          <br/>
          The GWAS z-score is <Katex tex="z_j = \sqrt{N} \hat{\beta}_j = \sqrt{N} \sum_{k=1}^M r_{jk} \beta_k + \epsilon'_j" /> with <Katex tex="\epsilon'_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1)" />.
          <br/><br/>
          Taking the expectation of <Katex tex="\chi^2_j = z_j^2" /> conditional on the LD structure:
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[z_j^2 \mid \ell_j] = N \sum_{k=1}^M r_{jk}^2 \text{Var}(\beta_k) + \text{Var}(\epsilon'_j) = N \left(\sum_{k=1}^M r_{jk}^2\right) \frac{h^2}{M} + 1 = 1 + \frac{N h^2}{M} \ell_j" display={true} />
          In the presence of uncorrected population stratification confounding contributing variance <Katex tex="a" />, this generalizes to <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\chi^2_j] = 1 + \frac{N h^2}{M}\ell_j + Na" />.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Cross-Trait Genetic Correlation (r_g)</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Bivariate Cross-Trait LD Score Regression</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[z_{1j} z_{2j}] = \frac{\sqrt{N_1 N_2} \rho_g}{M} \ell_j + \frac{\rho_0 N_s}{\sqrt{N_1 N_2}}, \qquad r_g = \frac{\rho_g}{\sqrt{h_1^2 h_2^2}}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="z_{1j}, z_{2j}" /> = Z-scores for trait 1 and trait 2 &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\rho_g" /> = Genetic covariance &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="N_s" /> = Sample overlap count &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\rho_0" /> = Phenotypic correlation among shared samples.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Stratified LDSC (S-LDSC, Finucane et al. 2015)</h3>
      <p>
        Partitions the genome into <Katex tex="C" /> overlapping functional annotations (e.g. cell-type-specific H3K27ac enhancers, conserved regions, coding exons):
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Stratified LDSC Model & Enrichment Statistic</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\chi^2_j] = 1 + N \sum_{c=1}^{C} \tau_c \ell(j, c) + N a, \qquad \text{Enrichment}(C) = \frac{h^2(C) / h^2}{|C| / M}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\ell(j, c) = \sum_{k \in C} r_{jk}^2" /> = Stratified LD score with respect to category <Katex tex="C" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\tau_c" /> = Specific per-SNP heritability contribution of category <Katex tex="c" />.
        </div>
      </div>

