    <section id="ch9">
      <h2>9. Causal Inference: Mendelian Randomization (MR)</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Mendelian Randomization (MR)</strong> uses genetic variants as <strong>Instrumental Variables (IVs)</strong> to test whether an observational exposure <Katex tex="X" /> causally influences a health outcome <Katex tex="Y" />.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-biology">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> Nature's Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)
        </div>
        <p>
          Because alleles are randomly segregated and assortment occurs at meiosis (Mendel's Law), genetic variants are fixed at conception and precede disease onset, naturally protecting against <strong>reverse causation</strong> and <strong>unmeasured environmental confounding</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>The Three Core Instrumental Variable (IV) Assumptions</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>1. Relevance Assumption:</strong> The genetic variant <Katex tex="Z" /> must be strongly associated with the exposure <Katex tex="X" /> (first-stage <Katex tex="F\text{-statistic} > 10" />).</li>
        <li><strong>2. Independence / Exchangeability Assumption:</strong> The variant <Katex tex="Z" /> must be independent of all unmeasured confounders <Katex tex="U" /> between exposure and outcome.</li>
        <li><strong>3. Exclusion Restriction:</strong> The variant <Katex tex="Z" /> affects the outcome <Katex tex="Y" /> <strong>strictly through exposure <Katex tex="X" /></strong> (no direct horizontal pleiotropy).</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>The Inverse-Variance Weighted (IVW) Estimator</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">IVW Fixed-Effects Causal Estimator</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{\text{MR}} = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{K} w_j \hat{\beta}_{Xj} \hat{\beta}_{Yj}}{\sum_{j=1}^{K} w_j \hat{\beta}_{Xj}^2}, \quad w_j = \frac{1}{\text{Var}(\hat{\beta}_{Yj})}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Robust Pleiotropy Methods</h3>
      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Method</th>
              <th>Mathematical Formulation</th>
              <th>Robustness Mechanism</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>MR-Egger Regression (Bowden 2015)</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{Yj} = \beta_0 + \beta_{\text{MR}} \hat{\beta}_{Xj} + \epsilon_j" /></td>
              <td>Intercept <Katex tex="\beta_0 \neq 0" /> tests for directional horizontal pleiotropy under the InSIDE assumption.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Weighted Median (Bowden 2016)</strong></td>
              <td>Median of sorted ratio estimates <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{Yj} / \hat{\beta}_{Xj}" /> with normalized weights.</td>
              <td>Consistent even if up to <strong>50% of the genetic weight comes from invalid instruments</strong>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>MR-PRESSO (Verbanck 2018)</strong></td>
              <td>Residual sum of squares outlier detection test.</td>
              <td>Identifies and removes individual pleiotropic outlier variants before recalculating IVW.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

