      <h2>10. The Modern Synthesis: Statistical Genetics Meets Deep Learning</h2>
      <p>
        Today, the frontier of statistical genetics is merging with sequence-to-function deep learning (e.g. <strong>Borzoi</strong>, <strong>AlphaGenome</strong>) and phylogeny-informed genomic language models (e.g. <strong>GPN-Star</strong>):
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Functionally Informed Fine-Mapping (Sniff / PolyFun):</strong> Deep learning predictions across 7,611 coverage tracks provide empirical priors (<Katex tex="\boldsymbol{\pi}" />) for SuSiE, boosting fine-mapping yield by +9.45%.</li>
        <li><strong>Deep Rare Variant Association Testing (DeepRVAT):</strong> Genomic language model representations replace crude frequency cutoffs, boosting WES power in UK Biobank.</li>
        <li><strong>Stratified LDSC with Learned Embeddings:</strong> Annotating genomes with continuous foundation model embeddings reveals novel tissue-specific trait heritability enrichments (<Katex tex="\tau^*" />).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Master Statistical Genetics Comparison Cheat-Sheet</h3>
      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Domain</th>
              <th>Flagship Method / Model</th>
              <th>Primary Mathematical Target</th>
              <th>Core Input Data Required</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Population Structure</strong></td>
              <td>PCA / Price et al. (2006)</td>
              <td>Eigenvectors of genotype covariance</td>
